1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,808
I scrub audio from cold cases. Today, a dead girl’s voice whispered my exact full name directly through my headphones.

2
00:00:09,108 --> 00:00:18,396
The cassette tape was recovered from a burnt cabin in 1999. It had been sitting sealed inside police evidence for decades.

3
00:00:18,696 --> 00:00:27,888
I ran the audio file through my modern restoration software, stripping away twenty-five years of hiss, rain, and digital rot.

4
00:00:28,188 --> 00:00:35,532
The muffled voice grew crystal clear: 'Marcus, he is already right behind you. Do not turn your head around.'

5
00:00:35,832 --> 00:00:43,488
My blood turned to pure ice. I tried telling myself it was just a cruel prank by another audio engineer.

6
00:00:43,788 --> 00:00:53,676
I isolated the background frequencies, listening past the girl's trembling breaths to clearly identify the perpetrator's movement.

7
00:00:53,976 --> 00:01:02,304
Underneath the harsh static, I heard a silver ring rhythmically tapping on desk wood—the exact pattern I tap while working.

8
00:01:02,604 --> 00:01:12,300
Terrified, I leaned into my studio microphone and asked, 'Who recorded this tape?' My own voice answered instantly from speakers.

9
00:01:12,600 --> 00:01:22,560
This was never an old tape from 1999. The experimental foam in this studio traps acoustic energy forward and backward through time.

10
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:32,316
A shadow stretched across my desk. I scrubbed crime audio for years, never realizing I was editing my own final moments.
